  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and embedded thunderstorms weaken \nand dissipate by sundown, with clearing skies overnight. Patchy fog \npossible.  \n\nUpper level low continues to pull away this afternoon into tonight. \nHowever scattered instability type showers have developed this \nafternoon in a weakly-unstable convective setting, with a couple \nbeing capable of a few lightning strikes. Expect activity to move \ngradually off to the south and east, with diminishing coverage and \nstrength as we move toward sunset. Severe weather is not expected, \nand even lightning will be tough to come by. We do clear out \ntonight, and with that comes the risk for patchy fog; the more \ntypically favored areas are most susceptible to patchy fog \ndevelopment. \n\nSaturday into Sunday otherwise looks favorable with full sun and \nseasonable temps, with seabreezes at the coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Slow-moving frontal boundary nearby Southern New \nEngland could favor several opportunities for showers and \nthunderstorms Monday through Wednesday, with street flooding \npossible. \n\nLatest ensembles point to an unsettled and potentially active early \nto midweek as a slow-moving frontal boundary remains nearby or over \nSouthern New England, with an influx of subtropical moisture on \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. Main risk this front will bring to \nSouthern New England is another period of soaking downpours given \nthe slow-moving frontal zone interacting with the highly moisture-\nrich airmass preceding it. It's still uncertain what the highest \nrain totals would look like and where they would fall, but with a \nfew spot areas in interior Southern New England having registered \nquite a bit of rain in the last 48 hours, there would be some risk \nfor localized street flooding. Probabilities for rainfall over 1\" in \n24 hours has increased into the lower to moderate (20-40%) range. \nSevere weather potential looks limited.",
